YMX License
===========

YMX is a streaming chiptune format for the Atari ST's 68000. This repository
holds the format specification (doc/SPEC.md, doc/BINARIES.md), the 68000
players, the Java and C# tools that pack and combine, and the test rigs. It
vendors the ST4 compression project (https://github.com/odipar/ST4) and,
through it, the ZX1 data compression format
(https://github.com/einar-saukas/ZX1), and it follows their dual licensing
structure:

1. THE COMPRESSOR - the compressors and optimizers in Java (org.st4) and C#
   (dotnet/st4), derived from Einar Saukas' optimal C compressor through
   ST1's jx1 - is available under the BSD 3-Clause License below. In
   practice this is relevant only if you modify its source code and/or
   incorporate the compressor within your own products; if you just execute
   it to pack files, you can simply ignore these conditions.

2. THE DECOMPRESSORS AND PLAYERS - the reference decompressors (Java
   org.st4.St4Decompressor and org.jx1, and their C# counterparts under
   dotnet/), the ST4 68000 decoders in 68k/ (ST4.S, ST4_wrap.S, ST4_ring.S),
   the YMX players (68k/YMX.S, 68k/YMX_sndh.S, 68k/YMX_player.S), and the
   prebuilt binaries the releases carry - can be used freely within your own
   programs (for any platform), even for commercial releases. The only
   condition is that you must indicate somehow in your documentation that
   you have used ZX1, via ST4 or YMX.

The YMX format itself may be implemented freely: doc/SPEC.md and
doc/BINARIES.md are the contract, and an independent writer, player or
combiner owes only the same acknowledgement.

Attribution
-----------

* The ZX1 data compression format and algorithm was designed and implemented
  by Einar Saukas (Copyright (c) 2021), with thanks to introspec/spke. The
  original C and z80 sources live in the ZX1 repository.
* The ST4 format (the four streams, the unit alignment, the container) is
  Copyright (c) 2026 Robbert van Dalen, vendored here as org.st4, dotnet/st4
  and the 68k/ decoders. ST4_wrap.S is based on ST1_wrap.S, which was
  written for ST1 by OpenAI Codex.
* The YMX format, the packers and combiners, the SNDH and PRG tooling, the
  C# tree and the emulation and measurement rigs are Copyright (c) 2026
  Robbert van Dalen; written by Claude (Anthropic's Claude Code) under
  Robbert van Dalen's direction.
* The YM5/YM6 register-dump formats are by Arnaud Carré (Leonard/Oxygene);
  YMX reads them, and the player was inspired by Steve Clarets' MinYMiser.
  The LHA depacker in org.ym6 and dotnet/ym6 is ported from the ST-Sound
  library's LZH code by Arnaud Carré, itself based on LZH code by Haruhiko
  Okumura (1991) and Kerwin F. Medina (1996).
* The .YMR register-dump format is the RhYMe tracker's export; the .ymr
  front end reads it, and ymr/CONVERSION.md records what the conversion
  keeps and loses.
* SNDH is the Atari ST scene's shared music container; the SNDH files YMX
  builds follow its v2 conventions.

Used by the build and the tests, not distributed
------------------------------------------------

* rmac, the 68000 assembler, assembles the players and prebuilt binaries.
* Hatari, with a TOS or EmuTOS image, runs the real-hardware harness.
* The Unicorn engine (GPL-2.0) is loaded at run time by the emulator rigs
  from the machine's own installed copy; nothing of it is vendored or
  distributed here.
* Maven pulls JSpecify, JUnit, Error Prone and NullAway for the Java build;
  the .NET SDK builds the C# tree. None are vendored.

